/**
 * PHASE-G0 — the EDITORIAL axis, which is not the runtime axis.
 * `ContentResourceStatus` above answers "is this version the one the runtime
 * serves?". This enum answers "has a human editorial process accepted it?".
 * They are deliberately independent, and `published` is deliberately NOT
 * reinterpreted as editorial approval: a version can be approved and not yet
 * bound to learners (the normal case, because approval must not publish), and a
 * historical version can be published without anyone ever having reviewed it
 * (every row that existed before this migration).
 * WHAT THE MIGRATION DOES WITH HISTORY. Every pre-existing row — published or
 * not — is defaulted to `draft`. That is the only honest option available:
 * there is no reviewer, no submission and no approval timestamp anywhere in the
 * database for those rows, so calling them `approved` would fabricate an
 * approval that never happened, and the ATA-100 package's own 154 editorial
 * gaps would silently read as closed. `draft` states the truth — "this content
 * exists and no editorial decision is recorded for it" — and costs nothing at
 * runtime, because the runtime reads `status`, never `editorialState`.
 */
enum EditorialState {
  draft
  submitted_for_review
  changes_requested
  approved
}

